WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.340
One way to learn the parameters of the neural network so that it gives you a good encoding for your pictures of faces

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.840
is to define and apply gradient descent on the triplet loss function.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.380
Let's see what that means.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.560
To apply the triplet loss, you need to compare pairs of images.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.060
For example, given this picture, to learn the parameters of the neural network,

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.360
you have to look at several pictures at the same time.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.920
For example, given this pair of images, you want their encodings to be similar because these are the same person.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:41.760
Whereas given this pair of images, you want their encodings to be quite different because these are different persons.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.960
In the terminology of the triplet loss, what you're going to do is always look at one anchor image,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:55.120
and then you want the distance between the anchor and a positive image, really a positive example,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.000
meaning it's the same person, to be similar.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:07.440
Whereas you want the anchor when paired or compared with a negative example for their distances to be much further apart.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:15.040
So this is what gives rise to the term triplet loss, which is that you'll always be looking at three images at a time.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:22.080
You'll be looking at an anchor image, a positive image, as well as a negative image.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:28.000
I'm going to abbreviate anchor, positive and negative as a, p, and n.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.080
So to formalize this, what you want is for the parameters of your neural network or for your encodings to have the following property,

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:44.800
which is that you want the encoding between the anchor minus the encoding of the positive example.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.960
You want this to be small.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:59.440
And in particular, you want this to be less than or equal to the distance or the squared norm between the encoding of the anchor and the encoding of the negative.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:08.160
Where of course this is d of a, p, and this is d of a, n.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.240
And you can think of d as a distance function, which is why we named it with the alphabet d.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:25.200
Now if we move the term from the right side of this equation to the left side, what you end up with is f of a minus f of p squared minus,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:31.360
I'm going to take the right hand side now, minus f of n squared.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.880
You want this to be less than or equal to zero.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:44.160
But now we're going to make a slight change to this expression, which is one trivial way to make sure this is satisfied is to just learn everything equals zero.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.640
If f always outputs zero, then this is zero minus zero, which is zero.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.400
This is zero minus zero, which is zero.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:03:01.520
And so, well, by saying f of any image equals a vector of all zeros, you can almost trivially satisfy this equation.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.360
So to make sure that the neural network doesn't just output zero for all the encodings,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.400
so to make sure that it doesn't set all the encodings equal to each other.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:20.160
Another way for the neural network to give a trivial output is if the encoding for every image was identical to the encoding to every other image,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.280
in which case you again get zero minus zero.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.280
So to prevent the neural network from doing that, what we're going to do is modify this objective

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:37.120
to say that this doesn't need to be just less than or equal to zero.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.640
It needs to be quite a bit smaller than zero.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:49.360
So in particular, we say this needs to be less than negative alpha, where alpha is another hyperparameter,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.520
then this prevents the neural network from outputting the trivial solutions.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.600
And by convention, usually we write plus alpha instead of negative alpha there.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:06.480
And this is also called a margin, which is terminology that you'd be familiar with.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:12.640
If you've also seen the literature on support vector machines, but don't worry about it if you haven't.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.000
And we can also modify this equation on top by adding this margin parameter.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.360
So to give an example, let's say the margin is set to 0.2.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:29.680
If in this example, d of the anchor and the positive is equal to 0.5,

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:35.200
then you won't be satisfied if d between the anchor and the negative was just a little bit bigger,

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.920
say 0.51.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.120
Even though 0.51 is bigger than 0.5, you're saying that's not good enough.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.160
We want d of a comma n to be much bigger than d of a comma p.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.440
And in particular, you want this to be at least 0.7 or higher.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.720
Or alternatively, to achieve this margin or this gap of at least 0.2,

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:06.240
you could either push this up or push this down so that there is at least this gap of this alpha,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:12.240
hyperparameter alpha at 0.2, between the distance between the anchor and the positive

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.480
versus the anchor and the negative.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.600
So that's what having a margin parameter here does,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:25.040
which is it pushes the anchor positive pair and the anchor negative pair further away from each other.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.200
So let's take this equation we have here at the bottom and on the next slide,

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.200
formalize it and define the triplet loss function.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.720
So the triplet loss function is defined on triples of images.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:48.960
So given three images, a, p, and n, the anchor positive and negative examples.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.280
So the positive examples is of the same person as the anchor,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.640
but the negative is of a different person than the anchor.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.440
We're going to define the loss as follows.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.680
The loss on this example, which is really defined on a triplet of images,

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.920
is let me first copy over what we had on the previous slide.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:25.170
So that was f a minus f p squared minus f a minus f n squared, and then plus alpha,

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:26.290
the margin parameter.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.930
And what you want is for this to be less than or equal to zero.

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:38.370
So to define the loss function, let's take the max between this and zero.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:44.770
So the effect of taking the max here is that so long as this is less than zero,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:51.890
then the loss is zero because the max is something less than equal to zero with zero is going to be

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:56.850
zero. So so long as you achieve the goal of making this thing of underline in green, so

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:01.650
long as you've achieved the objective of making that less than or equal to zero, then the loss

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:07.730
on this example is equal to zero. But if on the other hand, if this is greater than zero,

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:12.290
then if you take the max, the max will end up selecting this thing of underlying green.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:18.450
And so you would have a positive loss. So by trying to minimize this, this has the effect

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:24.770
of trying to send this thing to be zero or less than equal to zero. And then so long as this

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:30.450
zero or less than equal to zero, the neural network doesn't care how much further negative

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:36.930
negative it is. So this is how you define the loss on a single triplet. And the overall cost

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:44.610
function for your neural network can be some over a training set of these individual losses on

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:54.290
different triplets. So if you have a training set of, say 10,000 pictures with 1000 different

00:07:54.290 --> 00:08:01.970
persons, what you have to do is take your 10,000 pictures and use it to generate to select triplets

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:07.570
like this, and then train your learning algorithm using gradient descent on this type of cost

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:12.690
function, which is really defined on triplets of images drawn from your training set.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:22.850
Notice that in order to define this data set of triplets, you do need some pairs of A and P,

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:28.130
pairs of pictures of the same person. So the purpose of training your system, you do need

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:33.890
a data set where you have multiple pictures of the same person. That's why in this example,

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:40.210
I said if you have 10,000 pictures of 1000 different persons, so maybe you have 10 pictures

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:45.730
on average of each of your 1000 persons to make up your entire data set. If you had just one

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:51.330
picture of each person, then you can't actually train this system. But of course, after training,

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:57.570
if you're applying this, but of course, after having trained the system, you can then apply

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:03.810
it to your one shot learning problem where for your face recognition system, maybe you have only

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:08.850
a single picture of someone you might be trying to recognize. But for your training set, you do need

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:13.730
to make sure you have multiple images of the same person, at least for some people in your training

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:22.450
set, so you can have pairs of anchor and positive images. Now, how do you actually choose these

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:30.290
triplets to form your training set? One of the problems if you choose AP and N randomly from your

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:36.130
training set, subject to A and P being the same person and A and N being different persons,

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:42.290
one of the problems is that if you choose them sort of at random, then this constraint is very

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:50.850
easy to satisfy. Because given two randomly chosen pictures of people, chances are A and N are much

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:57.730
different than A and P. And I hope you still recognize this notation. This D, A, P was

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:04.450
what we had written on the last few slides as these encodings. So this is just equal to

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:11.010
this squared norm distance between the encodings that we had on the previous slide.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:16.210
But if A and N are two randomly chosen different persons, then there's a very high chance that

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:21.970
this will be much bigger, more than the margin alpha than that term on the left. And so the

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:26.770
neural network won't learn much from it. So to construct your training set, what you want to do

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:33.250
is to choose triplets, A, P and N, that are hard to train on. So in particular, what you want is

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:42.290
for all triplets that this constraint be satisfied. So a triplet that is hard would be if you

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:51.650
choose values for A, P and N, so that maybe D, A, P is actually quite close to D, A, N.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:58.610
So in that case, the learning algorithm has to try extra hard to take this thing on the right and

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:03.970
try to push it up or take this thing on the left and try to push it down so that there is

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:10.130
at least a margin of alpha between the left side and the right side. And the effect of choosing

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:16.370
these triplets is that it increases the computational efficiency of your learning algorithm.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:21.970
If you choose the triplets randomly, then too many triplets would be really easy. And so gradient

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:26.370
descent won't do anything because your neural network will just get them right pretty much

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:31.730
all the time. And it's only by choosing hard triplets that the gradient descent procedure

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:38.610
has to do some work to try to push these quantities further away from those quantities.

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:45.410
And if you're interested, the details are presented in this paper by Florinschoff,

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:51.330
Dimitri Kalenichenko and James Thoben, where they have a system called FaceNet,

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:56.770
which is where a lot of the ideas I'm presenting in this video had come from. By the way, this is

00:11:56.770 --> 00:12:02.530
also a fun fact about how algorithms are often named in the deep learning world, which is if

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:08.450
you work on a certain domain, let me call that blank, you often have a system called blank net

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:15.890
or deep blank. So we've been talking about face recognition. So this paper is called FaceNet.

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:23.570
And in the last video, you just saw deep face. But this idea of blank net or deep blank is a

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:30.290
very popular way of naming algorithms in the deep learning world. And you should feel free to take a

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:35.570
look at that paper if you want to learn some of these other details for speeding up your algorithm

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:41.330
by choosing the most useful triplets to train on. It is a nice paper. So just to wrap up,

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:47.570
to train on triplet loss, you need to take your training set and map it to a lot of triples. So

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:52.690
here is a triple with an anchor and a positive both of the same person and a negative of a

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:59.250
different person. Here's another one where the anchor and positive are of the same person,

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:05.170
but the anchor and negative are of different persons, and so on. And what you do having

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:11.010
to find this training set of anchor positive and negative triples is use gradient descent

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:18.370
to try to minimize the cost function J we defined on the earlier slide. And that will have the effect

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:25.410
of back propagating to all the parameters of the neural network in order to learn an encoding so

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:34.770
that D of two images will be small when these two images are of the same person. And there'll be

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:42.210
large when these are two images of different persons. So that's it for the triplet loss and

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:47.890
how you can use it to train a neural network to output a good encoding for face recognition.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:53.970
Now it turns out that today's face recognition systems, especially the large scale commercial

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:59.250
face recognition systems are trained on very large datasets. Datasets north of a million

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:04.450
images is not uncommon. Some companies are using north of 10 million images, and some companies

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:09.490
have north of 100 million images with which to try to train these systems. So these are very

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:15.090
large data sets, even by modern standards, these data set assets are not easy to acquire.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:22.290
Fortunately, some of these companies have trained these large networks and posted parameters online.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:27.170
So rather than trying to train one of these networks from scratch, this is one domain where

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:32.770
because of the sheer data volume sizes, this is one domain where often it might be useful for you

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:38.130
to download someone else's pre trained model rather than do everything from scratch yourself.

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:42.370
But even if you do download someone else's pre trained model, I think it's still useful

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:47.490
to know how these algorithms were trained in case you need to apply these ideas from scratch

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:53.010
yourself for some application. So that's it for the triplet loss. In the next video,

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:58.130
I want to show you also some other variations on Siamese networks and how to train these

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:00.563
systems. Let's go on to the next video.
